This is a case study in AI-driven drug repurposing: one patient, one model, one drug that was already sitting in pharmacies. It's also the real story behind a post that went viral with one basic detail wrong. Here's the documented version, from the people who treated him.

## Background: a disease that kept coming back

The patient, identified in public materials as Alan, was 50 years old and had spent two years fighting idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease, iMCD for short, a rare disorder in which the immune system attacks the body's own organs. Five different treatment regimens, BTK inhibitors, immune-suppressing drugs, anti-cancer agents, had failed him in turn. By the time his family was preparing for hospice care, he had relapsed for the fifth time.

His case was treated by Luke Chen, a hematologist at Vancouver General Hospital, working with David Fajgenbaum's team at the University of Pennsylvania. Their account was later published as a peer-reviewed letter in the New England Journal of Medicine.

## The method: scoring every drug against every disease

Fajgenbaum's team had built a machine learning model called KGML-xDTD, trained on a knowledge graph connecting drugs, diseases, and biology. Instead of starting from a hypothesis about one drug, it generates a predicted score for how likely every FDA-approved drug is to treat every disease, roughly 4,000 drugs, run against thousands of conditions at once.

Run against iMCD, the model ranked adalimumab, a drug approved for rheumatoid arthritis and Crohn's disease, as the top candidate. Nobody had used it for Castleman disease before.

## The intervention and outcome

Chen added adalimumab to Alan's sixth treatment regimen, alongside the BTK inhibitor and immune-suppressing drugs he was already on. Within days, his organs started working again and his symptoms eased.

{% statCallout
   number="22 months"
   label="Length of Alan's remission after adalimumab was added to his treatment, the longest since his diagnosis (Ma, Koslicki, Chen, Fajgenbaum et al., NEJM, 2025)." /%}

As of the February 2025 publication, he had been in remission for 22 months, longer than any stretch since his diagnosis.

## Why it worked: the biology behind the ranking

The prediction wasn't a guess with no explanation attached. It pointed researchers toward a specific mechanism: tumor necrosis factor, or TNF, a protein that adalimumab blocks. When Fajgenbaum's team checked blood samples from iMCD patients, the ones with the most severe disease had unusually high TNF activity, a biological reason the ranking might actually hold.

{% callout type="summary" %}
The model didn't invent a new drug. It noticed a connection between an old one and a disease it had never been tested on, and pointed to a biological reason why that connection might hold.
{% /callout %}

## From one case to a platform

Alan's case became the origin story for Every Cure, the nonprofit Fajgenbaum co-founded. Its platform, MATRIX, now scores roughly 75 million drug-disease combinations, backed by $48.3 million from the US government's ARPA-H and $60 million from the Audacious Project. A public version of the tool is expected in 2026. The team has more than 40 data scientists, engineers, and physicians working through it, with a stated goal of validating 15 to 25 repurposed treatments by 2030.

Early candidates under study include bosutinib for ALS, metreleptin for severe anorexia, and leucovorin for some cases of autism spectrum disorder. None of those are confirmed the way Alan's case is. They're hypotheses the model has flagged, the same starting point adalimumab was at before anyone tried it.

{% callout type="tip" %}
A post about this case went viral on X, describing the patient as 37 years old and saying he responded within a week. The documented record says he was 50, and that a week-long response came after two years and five failed regimens, not instead of them. Worth checking the original source before sharing a number.
{% /callout %}

## Limits of this case study

One documented case isn't a clinical trial. Twenty-two months is a real and meaningful remission, and it's also a number measured at the time of publication, not a guarantee the disease won't return, the way it returned five times before. The honest version of this story is a genuine success built on real biology, with real uncertainty still attached. Both things are true at once.

## Why this is worth knowing as a student, not just a headline

The habit that actually matters here isn't "trust the AI." It's the one Fajgenbaum's team used before they trusted it: they had a prediction, and they went looking for the biology that would explain it, elevated TNF levels in the sickest patients, before anyone gave a patient the drug. The model pointed somewhere. The reasoning is what made it safe to act on.

That's the same habit worth building with anything an AI tells you while you're still a student, a viral post, a chatbot answer, a study tool. Check where it came from. Ask what would have to be true for it to be right. A result without a reason behind it is a guess wearing a lab coat.

{% actionBox title="Build that habit here first" %}
Most AI tools students use weren't built for medicine. They answer from the open internet, and when they don't actually know something, they still answer anyway, confidently, and sometimes wrong. You end up trusting a result with no reason attached, the exact thing Fajgenbaum's team was careful not to do.

